{
 "federal": {
  "eligibility": "You must be at least 18 when you file and have been a lawful permanent resident (green card holder) for 5 years under INA 316, or 3 years if you are married to and living with a U.S. citizen spouse under INA 319(a). You must have lived continuously in the United States for that same 5 or 3 years, been physically present for at least 30 months out of the last 5 years (or 18 months out of the last 3 years for spouses), and lived at least 3 months in the state or USCIS district where you file. You must also show good moral character for the required period up to the oath (INA 316(a)(3)), pass English and civics tests (INA 312), and be willing to take the Oath of Allegiance and support the Constitution. You may file up to 90 days before you complete the continuous-residence requirement (INA 334).",
  "steps": "1) Confirm you meet the eligibility rules above and gather your green card, tax returns, and travel history. 2) File Form N-400 online through a USCIS online account ($710) or by paper ($760); if you are asking for a fee waiver or reduced fee, you must file by paper. 3) Attend a biometrics appointment (fingerprints and photo) if USCIS schedules one. 4) Study for the English and civics tests, then attend your naturalization interview at a USCIS field office, where an officer reviews your application and tests you. 5) If approved, receive Form N-445, attend your oath ceremony, return your green card, take the Oath of Allegiance, and receive your Certificate of Naturalization.",
  "fee_n400_online": 710,
  "fee_n400_paper": 760,
  "fee_source_url": "https://www.uscis.gov/n-400",
  "fee_waiver": "You can ask USCIS to waive the N-400 fee entirely by filing Form I-912 (there is no fee to file I-912) with a paper N-400; you cannot file online if you request a waiver. You qualify if you receive a means-tested benefit, your household income is at or below 150% of the Federal Poverty Guidelines (for example, $21,870 for a household of 1 in the contiguous United States under the guidelines effective January 13, 2026), or you can show financial hardship. The N-400 fee remains waiver-eligible even though H.R.-1 (Pub. L. 119-21, July 4, 2025) made some other USCIS fees non-waivable.",
  "reduced_fee": "If your household income is more than 150% but not more than 400% of the Federal Poverty Guidelines, you may qualify for a reduced N-400 fee of $380 by filing Form I-942, which is now Part 10 of Form N-400. Like a fee waiver, a reduced-fee request must be filed by paper, not online. See the USCIS reduced-fee page and the I-942 page for the current income tables.",
  "exemptions": "If you are 50 or older and have been a lawful permanent resident for 20 years (50/20), or 55 or older with 15 years as a resident (55/15), you are exempt from the English test but must still take the civics test, which you may take in your own language with an interpreter. If you are 65 or older with 20 years as a resident (65/20), you also get a shorter civics test drawn from a special list of 20 questions. If a medically determinable physical, developmental, or mental disability lasting or expected to last at least 12 months prevents you from meeting the English and/or civics requirement, a licensed medical professional can certify the exception on Form N-648, explaining how the disability prevents you from meeting the requirement.",
  "tests": "The English test has three parts: reading (read one of three sentences aloud), writing (write one of three sentences correctly), and speaking and understanding, which the officer assesses throughout the interview. The civics test depends on when you filed: if you filed BEFORE October 20, 2025, you take the 2008 test, drawn from 100 questions, with the officer asking up to 10 and 6 correct answers needed to pass (5 wrong answers is a fail). If you filed ON OR AFTER October 20, 2025, you take the 2025 test, drawn from 128 questions, with the officer asking 20 and 12 correct answers needed to pass (9 wrong answers is a fail). Applicants age 65 or older with 20 years as a resident (65/20) answer 10 questions from a special bank of 20 under either version. If you fail any part at your first interview, USCIS schedules a re-examination 60 to 90 days later covering only the part you failed; failing a second time means your application is denied, though you may apply again later with a new fee.",
  "timeline": "USCIS publishes processing-time estimates by field office in its Case Processing Times tool, updated monthly, showing the time in which it completed 80% of adjudicated cases over the prior six months, from receipt to decision. There is no single national figure, so check your own field office in the tool. As of the USCIS data checked November 14, 2026, the national median was about 7.4 months for fiscal year 2026 through August 30, 2026, with published office ranges roughly 8 to 17.5 months for 80% of cases and the slowest offices over 20 months; do not treat any single number as a guarantee for your case.",
  "after_approval": "You are not a U.S. citizen until you take the Oath of Allegiance at a naturalization ceremony (run by USCIS or by a court). USCIS sends Form N-445 telling you when and where to appear; at the ceremony you check in, return your Permanent Resident Card (green card), take the oath, and receive your Certificate of Naturalization, which you should check for errors before you leave. After the ceremony, update your Social Security record with the Social Security Administration (generally wait at least 10 days, or ask USCIS to update it when you file the N-400), apply for a U.S. passport, and register to vote where you are eligible; a voter registration application is often provided at the ceremony.",
  "sources": [
   "https://www.uscis.gov/n-400",
   "https://www.uscis.gov/citizenship/learn-about-citizenship/10-steps-to-naturalization",
   "https://www.uscis.gov/citizenship/find-study-materials-and-resources/study-for-the-test",
   "https://www.uscis.gov/forms/filing-fees/poverty-guidelines",
   "https://www.uscis.gov/i-912",
   "https://www.uscis.gov/i-942",
   "https://www.uscis.gov/citizenship/learn-about-citizenship/naturalization-ceremonies",
